{"name":"Fabric of Space","hex":"#341758","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fabric","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edifice or building.","The act of constructing, construction, fabrication."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with fabric."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fabric"},{"term":"space","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Unlimited or generalized extent, physical or otherwise.","A physical extent across two or three dimensions (sometimes for or to do something)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To roam, walk, wander.","To set some distance apart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/space"}],"entries":[{"title":"Space","blocks":[{"text":"Space is a three-dimensional continuum containing positions and directions. In classical physics, physical space is often conceived in three linear dimensions. Modern physicists usually consider it, with time, to be part of a boundless four-dimensional continuum known as spacetime. The concept of space is considered to be of fundamental importance to an understanding of the physical universe. However, disagreement continues between philosophers over whether it is itself an entity, a relationship between entities, or part of a conceptual framework."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/3D_coordinate_system.svg/330px-3D_coordinate_system.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/500px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/1280px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Franck